{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785260962,"nonce":"MZSXA4I3sLYeu/TmvSIO89JR4yA=","title":"ADR-0065 s3: the widened-rerun affordance must name the WHOLE cascade, not its start point","message":"Re-derivation is human-triggered — ADR-0061 already specifies that \"expired inputs widen a rerun's scope and say so\", with affordances that state which they are (\"Rerun this step\" vs \"Inputs expired — this re-runs from `clone`\"). Losing a snapshot to eviction is the same event with a different cause, so **no new Attempt outcome is needed** and no purity property is needed in the engine.\n\n**But the hazard moves rather than vanishing.** Widening to `build` cascades to descendants under ADR-0027. On `build → deploy-staging → push`, retrying `push` with expired inputs drags `deploy-staging` with it and **re-deploys staging as a side effect of a retry**.\n\nThe answer is disclosure, not prevention: the human decides, and can only decide if told. This is ADR-0027's \"smart never means mysterious\" applied to blast radius.\n\n- [ ] The affordance names every step the widening will re-run, not just where it starts — \"this re-runs from `build`, which also re-runs `deploy-staging`\"\n- [ ] Distinguish \"inputs expired\" from \"inputs evicted\" only if it changes what the user would do; otherwise one message\n- [ ] The scope shown is computed from the same invalidation set the engine will actually use, not a separate estimate that can drift","files":null}]}